"first\" and nobody fixes the hundred and twentieth file. ``0`` means all "
"of them, and ``--format json`` always carries all of them whatever this "
"says."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.FORMATS:1 of
msgid ""
"What a rendering may be asked for. ``json`` is the model itself, and is "
"first because it is the one that outlives this file."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.GATE_DOC_LABEL:3 of
msgid ""
"An absolute URL is right in a README and on a standalone page, and wrong "
"inside the documentation: a reader on ``/en/latest/…`` who follows one "
"lands in a *different version* of the site, and a page that is new on "
"this branch has no other version to land in at all. A ``:ref:`` resolves "
"inside whatever build it is in — every version, every translation, and an"
" offline build with no network — and cannot 404. The label is defined at "
"the top of ``docs/source/guides/devops/the-gate.rst``."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.GATE_DOC_URL:4 of
msgid ""
"``latest`` and not ``stable``, which is the one difference from the "
"grants report's link and is not a preference. ``stable`` is the last "
"*release*; this page describes the gate as it is now, and a repository "
"whose gate changed since its last release would link its README at a page"
" that either does not exist yet — which is what happened, a 404 reached "
"from the documentation itself — or describes rules nobody is being held "
"to. ``latest`` tracks the branch the figures were measured on, which is "
"the same thing the report is about."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.GATE_DOC_URL freeports_dev.ci.render.SCRIPT
#: freeports_dev.ci.render.STYLE of
msgid "type"
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.GATE_DOC_URL:11 of
msgid "Where a reader goes to find out what any of this is worth"
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.INCONCLUSIVE:4 of
msgid ""
"Grey, and never a pass. A figure nobody could compute is not a figure "
"above the threshold — the rule :mod:`freeports_dev.ci.gate` already "
"applies, and the same one the grants report applies to a methodology page"
" it could not reach."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.MARKERS:1 of
msgid ""
"The lines a rewritable file must already contain, per format. Ordinary "
"comments in both languages, so a file carrying them renders exactly as it"
" did before anything was written between them. They say ``freeports-dev``"
" where the grants report says ``freeports-validate``: one file may well "
"carry both blocks, and a marker that did not name its writer would have "
"them overwrite each other."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.MarkersMissing:3 of
msgid ""
"Deliberately not \"append it at the end\". Where a table belongs in a "
"README is a judgement about that README, and a command that guessed would"
" put one under a licence notice once and then keep it there for ever."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.NOTICE:3 of
msgid ""
"A page of percentages invites exactly one misreading — that somebody has "
"certified the software — and a coverage figure is the easiest number in "
"this project to mistake for a guarantee. What a report shows is what one "
"run measured, on one machine, with whatever tools that machine had. It is"
" evidence a reader can reproduce, and the only way to be sure of a line "
"in it is to re-run the command that measured it."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.SCRIPT:1 of
msgid ""
"switching viewpoint, and filtering rows by substring. Written out rather "
"than pulled from a framework for the same reason as the stylesheet, and "
"kept small enough to read in one screen."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.SCRIPT:5 of
msgid "The only behaviour the page has"
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.STYLE:6 of
msgid ""
"A near-copy of the one `freeports-validate report` writes, and "
"deliberately not shared with it: the two packages are installed "
"independently, and a formats repository may hold one and not the other. A"
" stylesheet is not worth a dependency between two commands that otherwise"
" never import each other."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.STYLE:11 of
msgid ""
"Everything the page needs to look like a page. Inline, and it has to stay"
" inline"
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.TABLES:1 of
msgid ""
"The three values of ``--table``. ``summary`` is first because it is the "
"default and the one a README carries; the other two are the two questions"
" it provokes — *what is being asked of this repository*, and *where does "
"a figure that fell short actually come from*."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.TOTAL_KEY:1 of
msgid ""
"How each verdict is counted in ``totals``. A key rather than the verdict "
"itself, because ``no threshold`` carries a space and a JSON key somebody "
"has to quote in ``jq`` is a key somebody gets wrong; ``ungated`` says the"
" same thing in one word."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.VERDICT_LABEL:1 of
msgid ""
"How each verdict is written outside the terminal. ``ci-check``'s own "
"labels shout, which is right on a console at the moment a commit is "
"refused and wrong in a document somebody reads later."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.VIEWPOINTS:1 of
msgid ""
"The three tabs, which are the three tables. A reader who has learned the "
"`--table` names has learned the page, and a page that invented a fourth "
"arrangement would be teaching a second vocabulary for the same run."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.badge_name:3 of
msgid ""
"The same mechanical rule :func:`freeports_dev.ci.report.file_name` uses "
"for the measurement files, so a person who knows the metric can find both"
" without a lookup table."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.build:3 of
msgid ""
"It carries what the gate decided *and* what the registry knows about each"
" metric — its cost class and the sentence saying what it measures. A "
"table reading ``docs.rust 39.4 %`` and nothing else tells a reader a "
"number and not what was counted, and the reader of a documentation page "
"has no ``--help`` to hand."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.explained:3 of
msgid ""
"The sentences are the part that matters. A table of verdicts tells "
"somebody a figure is wrong; a sentence naming the tool, the number and "
"the command tells them what to do about it."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.failing_conditions:3 of
msgid ""
"They are not metrics: they have no unit, no minimum and nothing to plot, "
"so every rendering prints them as sentences under the table rather than "
"inventing table cells for them."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.metric_badge:3 of
msgid ""
"**The colour is the figure, not the verdict** — with two exceptions. A "
"figure under this repository's own minimum is red whatever its value, "
"because falling short of a rule the repository wrote down is news; and "
"one nobody could compute, or one taken at another commit, is grey, "
"because it is not a figure about this code at all."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.metric_badge:8 of
#, python-format
msgid ""
"Everywhere else the colour is the absolute number. Painting 32 % green on"
" the grounds that 32 is what this repository currently demands would "
"flatter a reader with the project's own floor, and a badge exists to be "
"read by somebody who has not opened `ci.yaml`."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.render:1 of
msgid ""
"One rendering of the model, as text. ``badges`` is not here: it is "
"several files."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.render:3 of
msgid ""
"``--table`` chooses among the three arrangements, and only the two text "
"formats have it. ``json`` is the model and a table of it would be a "
"second, lossy one; ``html`` already carries all three as tabs, so asking "
"it for one would be asking for a page with two tabs removed. Both refuse "
"rather than ignore the argument — silently rendering something other than"
" what was asked for is how a hook writes the wrong file for a year."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.render_badges:3 of
msgid ""
"One for the run as a whole, one for the test suites, and one for every "
"metric that has something to say — measured, or gated, or both. A metric "
"that is neither is left out: a wall of ``n/a`` badges in a README says "
"nothing about the repository and hides the ones that do."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.rewrite:3 of
msgid ""
"The markers are found in ``block`` itself rather than passed separately, "
"so a block can only ever be written into a file marked for that same "
"format. Idempotent by construction: what is written between the markers "
"includes them, and what is replaced is everything from one to the other."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.status_of:3 of
msgid ""
"``failing`` first: a figure known to be under its minimum, or a suite "
"known to have broken, is the strongest thing this run can say, and one "
"unmeasured metric beside it does not soften that. ``inconclusive`` "
"second, and it covers the skipped figures too — a metric a ``--skip-"
"slow`` run never looked at must not be mistaken afterwards for one that "
"passed."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.status_of:8 of
msgid ""
"**A suite that is stale or was never run is inconclusive, not failing**, "
"and the difference is the whole vocabulary of this report: `failing` says"
" something is wrong with the code, and sending somebody to look for a "
"broken test that does not exist is how a status word stops being read. "
"Stale says something is unknown, which is a different thing to do about "
"it — run it — and it is exactly as far from `passing`."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.status_of:14 of
msgid ""
"**A run that gated nothing is inconclusive, not passing.** \"Passing\" "
"means every gated figure cleared its minimum, and with no minima there is"
" nothing that cleared anything — a green badge on a repository nobody has"
" configured is the vacuous truth this whole mechanism exists to keep out "
"of a README. It is also the first state of every repository `init-format-"
"repo` creates, and it says so."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.suites_badge:3 of
msgid ""
"The badge the fast/slow split makes necessary. A gate that runs the cheap"
" suites at every commit publishes a green tick that a reader will take to"
" mean \"the tests pass\" — and it means \"the tests we could afford "
"pass\". This says which, in the README, without anybody having to open "
"the report: *all ran*, or how many broke, or how many are stale or were "
"never run here."
msgstr ""

#: freeports_dev.ci.render.suites_badge:8 of
msgid ""
"Broken is red and unknown is grey, the same vocabulary the metric badges "
"use, and for the same reason: a suite nobody ran is not a suite that "
"passed, and it is not a suite that failed either."
msgstr ""

